1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,200
 Alright, having talked about a few different types of composites, we're now going to deep dive into a very specific area.

2
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:10,400
 We're going to talk over the next few videos about fiber reinforced polymers.

3
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:16,200
 Last week we talked about what it is that makes up a fiber reinforced polymer and how these things tend to get their properties,

4
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:20,000
 but we didn't talk a whole lot about why it is you might choose to use one.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,000
 And there are many different reasons why you might choose to use a fiber reinforced polymer for a job,

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:28,200
 but one of the main ones is the versatility in manufacturing that they provide.

7
00:00:28,200 --> 00:00:33,600
 In some cases, fiber reinforced polymers are the only sensible approach to achieving a particular geometry.

8
00:00:33,600 --> 00:00:39,800
 Say you have some really interesting complex organic geometry that needs to be stiff and strong and lightweight.

9
00:00:39,800 --> 00:00:42,600
 The aerodynamic surface is on a Formula One car, for example.

10
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:45,400
 And in other cases, kind of on the other end of the spectrum,

11
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:51,400
 sometimes fiber reinforced polymers are the only way that's economical or practical to achieve a particular geometry.

12
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:56,400
 Say if you have some really, really large but thin body like a boat hull or a pool.

13
00:00:56,400 --> 00:01:07,600
 Another benefit in manufacturing a part out of a fiber reinforced polymer is that you can often optimize the properties of that part to work in concert with the geometry for a very specific load case.

14
00:01:07,600 --> 00:01:11,800
 You can do this both by aligning the fibers in the expected direction of load,

15
00:01:11,800 --> 00:01:19,400
 but also by placing extra reinforcement, extra fibers in areas that you expect are going to carry a huge amount of load.

16
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:29,200
 And this versatility that you get from manufacturing, this ability that you have to finely tune the properties mean that fiber reinforced polymers can really be min-max materials.

17
00:01:29,200 --> 00:01:33,200
 In circumstances where you know where you can predict the loading scenario really, really well,

18
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:39,800
 you can get really, really lightweight, really, really volume efficient solutions to manufacturing a particular part.

19
00:01:39,800 --> 00:01:44,300
 Over the next few videos, we're going to look in detail at how fiber reinforced polymers are made.

20
00:01:44,300 --> 00:01:50,900
 We're going to start by talking about how the constituent components are produced, the fibers themselves and the reasons that are used to lay them up.

21
00:01:50,900 --> 00:01:56,600
 We're going to talk a little bit about the molds and forms the geometry upon which you build your fiber reinforced polymers.

22
00:01:56,600 --> 00:01:59,400
 And then we're going to talk specifically about the layout process.

23
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:04,600
 In any case, we're probably going to go a little bit more in detail here than any manufacturing process we've met so far.

24
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:09,200
 Partly because this is a super interesting process, but also because the details matter a lot here.

25
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:17,700
 In order for you to design a part that's going to be made from a fiber reinforced polymer, you need to have a really, really good understanding of the entire process, the way that this is made.

26
00:02:17,700 --> 00:02:25,600
 Because without this understanding, without paying considerations toward this manufacturing you might design in features that are impossible to make using an FRP.

27
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:35,800
 I focus over the next couple of videos is going to be primarily on layout composites on laminated FRP's, but we will at the end of this talk about embedded FRP's as well, plastics that have some more amounts of fiber within them.

28
00:02:35,800 --> 00:02:40,700
 On note from the outset that this is a highly technical process and I am by no means an expert on it.

29
00:02:40,700 --> 00:02:47,600
 I've done my fair share of fiberglass and carbon fiber layout before both for my own projects and as part of the Formula SAE team here on campus.

30
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:57,300
 But thankfully, a composites manufacturing expert, Professor Bill McBride has agreed to come onto the live show with us this week and talk a little bit about his 30 plus years of experience working with composites.
